# base/tiers/s2s3 -- S3's PROPAGATION KERNELS.
#
# §8 tier 6. These are what make the constant table an INDUCTION rather than a
# list: given EX/UNIQ/CONG for F and H, the composite G(x,r) := exists t. (F(x,t)
# & H(t,r)) inherits all three. Parametric in F and H, so they are proved once
# here and instantiated per constant.
#
# WHY compose_uniq NEEDS CONG(H), which is why S2 lists three obligations and
# not two: given G(x,r) and G(x,r') the intermediates t, t' DIFFER. UNIQ(F)
# gives eq(t,t'), but UNIQ(H) is stated at a single t, so it cannot be applied
# until CONG(H) transports H(t',r') to H(t,r'). §8 records a negative control:
# supplying eq(t,t') where eq(t',t) is needed fails at implies_elim.
#
# `the_EX` / `the_UNIQ` WERE HERE AND ARE DELETED -- do not reinstate them.
# They proved EX/UNIQ for the empty-default description
#     D(r) := (U & P(r)) | (~U & r is empty),   U := "exactly one set satisfies P"
# on the reading that FOUNDATION §2 S3 makes `The` a BASE CASE. It is not one in
# this export: Isabelle DEFINES The(P) == Union(Replace({0}, %x y. P(y))), so
# `The` reduces through zero/cons/Replace/Union by the induction step above, and
# `walk` emits its four claims. Nothing ever cited the two schemas. The only
# thing they were reachable for was rendering a constant's @def AS D -- which
# needs no class and no Pair graph, checks GREEN, and is an N2 violation, since
# the @def then no longer says what Isabelle says. `constant::description_tests`
# proves `The(C,r) <-> D(r)` instead, with congruence for P as a hypothesis.
#
# `lem` stays -- `proof_builder_tests` instantiates it -- but nothing in this
# unit uses it any more, and `use empty` is now absent from the whole tier.
#
# ALL FOUR ARE INTERLEAVED -- `forall x. (set(x) -> (forall t. ...))`, never the
# grouped `forall x. forall t. (set(x) -> (set(t) -> ...))`. compose_uniq and
# compose_cong used to be GROUPED while compose_ex and replace_fun were not, and
# the two shapes are different formulas to the kernel: a guarded obligation in
# one shape cannot be fed to a kernel stated in the other. That inconsistency
# was invisible for as long as nothing instantiated the kernels.
#
# The statements therefore DIVERGE from the `constants_s2s3` probe, which keeps
# the grouped forms; the proofs below are the probe's with the guards
# re-threaded. FOUNDATION §1 R3 fixes the shape.

schema lem [Q(0)]: (Q | (~ Q))
schema compose_ex [F(2), H(2)]: ((forall x. (set(x) -> (exists t. (set(t) & F(x,t))))) -> ((forall t. (set(t) -> (exists r. (set(r) & H(t,r))))) -> (forall x. (set(x) -> (exists r. (set(r) & (exists t. (set(t) & (F(x,t) & H(t,r))))))))))
schema compose_uniq [F(2), H(2)]: ((forall x. (set(x) -> (forall t. (set(t) -> (forall t2. (set(t2) -> (F(x,t) -> (F(x,t2) -> eq(t,t2))))))))) -> ((forall t. (set(t) -> (forall r. (set(r) -> (forall r2. (set(r2) -> (H(t,r) -> (H(t,r2) -> eq(r,r2))))))))) -> ((forall a. (set(a) -> (forall b. (set(b) -> (forall r. (set(r) -> (eq(a,b) -> (H(a,r) -> H(b,r))))))))) -> (forall x. (set(x) -> (forall r. (set(r) -> (forall r2. (set(r2) -> ((exists t. (set(t) & (F(x,t) & H(t,r)))) -> ((exists t. (set(t) & (F(x,t) & H(t,r2)))) -> eq(r,r2))))))))))))
schema compose_cong [F(2), H(2)]: ((forall a. (set(a) -> (forall b. (set(b) -> (forall t. (set(t) -> (eq(a,b) -> (F(a,t) -> F(b,t))))))))) -> (forall a. (set(a) -> (forall b. (set(b) -> (forall r. (set(r) -> (eq(a,b) -> ((exists t. (set(t) & (F(a,t) & H(t,r)))) -> (exists t. (set(t) & (F(b,t) & H(t,r)))))))))))))

# **Replacement's side condition, discharged once for every use of `Replace`.**
#
# Isabelle defines `Replace(A,P) == PrimReplace(A, %x y. (EX! z. P(x,z)) & P(x,y))`,
# and `PrimReplace_EX` is hypothesis-strengthened on `Fun(F)` because axiom 16 is.
# The `EX!` wrapper is exactly what discharges it: whatever Psi is, the wrapped
# graph is single-valued, because both candidates equal the unique z.
#
# Parametric in Psi and therefore legitimate under R2: `F` is a PARAMETER
# constrained by a hypothesis, not a class built from an uninterpreted
# predicate. Nothing here constructs {x : Psi(x)} -- the caller hands F over
# from `class/`, and this only reads the biconditional.
#
# BOTH SHAPES ARE INTERLEAVED (`forall x. (set(x) -> (forall y. ...)))`), and
# that is not cosmetic. The hypothesis must match `class::compile_at`'s emitted
# claim and the conclusion must match `PrimReplace_EX`'s antecedent, both of
# which interleave binder and guard. The grouped form
# `forall x. forall y. (set(x) -> (set(y) -> ...))` is logically identical and
# `implies_elim` rejects it at both joins.
schema replace_fun [Psi(2)]: (forall F. ((forall x. (set(x) -> (forall y. (set(y) -> (pmem(x,y,F) <-> ((exists z. (set(z) & (Psi(x,z) & (forall w. (set(w) -> (Psi(x,w) -> eq(z,w))))))) & Psi(x,y))))))) -> (forall u. (set(u) -> (forall v. (set(v) -> (forall v2. (set(v2) -> (pmem(u,v,F) -> (pmem(u,v2,F) -> eq(v,v2)))))))))))

# The single-valuedness guard a Replace-shaped body carries is DERIVABLE when
# Psi encodes a function, and dropping it is what keeps class compilation
# linear: `Replace` filters to the single-valued case by conjoining
# `exists! z. Psi(u,z)` at every membership site, so an inlined body repeats
# Psi THREE times -- existence, uniqueness, witness -- and a k-deep nest of
# inlinings costs 3^k. With this schema the guard is discharged once and the
# other two copies go, so the multiplier is 1 and the nest is linear in k.
#
# Parametric in Psi and therefore legitimate under R2: Psi is never
# interpreted, and nothing here builds {x : Psi(x)}. The premises are exactly
# S2's EX and UNIQ for Psi, which a hoisted lambda-class gets from
# compose_ex/compose_uniq above.
schema guard_from_ex_uniq [Psi(2)]: ((forall u. (set(u) -> (exists r. (set(r) & Psi(u,r))))) -> ((forall u. (set(u) -> (forall r. (set(r) -> (forall r2. (set(r2) -> (Psi(u,r) -> (Psi(u,r2) -> eq(r,r2))))))))) -> (forall u. (set(u) -> (exists z. (set(z) & ((exists v. (set(v) & (Psi(u,v) & eq(z,v)))) & (forall w. (set(w) -> ((exists v. (set(v) & (Psi(u,v) & eq(w,v)))) -> eq(z,w)))))))))))

# A TUPLED CONSTANT IS PARTIAL. R7 makes a constant unary on ONE tuple, and
# FOUNDATION §1a makes the tuple a function: `C(t,r)` is FALSE at every `t`
# that is not a tuple of its arity.
#
# WHY `compose_ex_dom` EXISTS. `compose_ex` requires its `H` total on EVERY set,
# which a tupled constant is not, so `compose_ex` cannot consume one. The
# domain-restricted form takes `F` landing in `D` and `H` total ON `D`. `D` is
# never interpreted and nothing builds `{p : D(p)}`, so R2 holds.
#
# `pair_fn_*` and `tuple_*` WERE HERE AND ARE DELETED: they tupled through
# Kuratowski pairs, `kpair(a,b,p)`, the nested tuple §1a rules out. The
# function-tuple's own lemmas replace them; do not reinstate these.
schema compose_ex_dom [F(2), D(1), H(2)]: ((forall x. (set(x) -> (exists t. (set(t) & (F(x,t) & D(t)))))) -> ((forall t. (set(t) -> (D(t) -> (exists r. (set(r) & H(t,r)))))) -> (forall x. (set(x) -> (exists r. (set(r) & (exists t. (set(t) & (F(x,t) & H(t,r))))))))))
